True. When I wrote the comment I did not think about the Vec though.<p>The point I am trying to make is more general:<p>I believe that when you have a type in Rust that is not Copy it will never be implicitly copied in a way that you end up with two visible instances <i>but</i> it is <i>not</i> guaranteed that Rust never implicitly memcopies <i>all</i> its bytes.<p>I have not tried it but what I had in mind instead of the Vec was a big struct that is not Copy. Something like:<p><pre><code>   struct Big&lt;const M: usize&gt; {
       buf: [u8; M],
   }

   &#x2F;&#x2F; Make it non-Copy.
   impl&lt;const M: usize&gt; Drop for Big&lt;M&gt; {
        fn drop(&amp;mut self) {} 
   }
</code></pre>
From my understanding, to know if memory is shoveled around it is not enough to know the function signature and whether the type is Copy or not. The specifics of the type matter.
